te has no piece on d4 or f4. White has a pawn on e2 (PWF0), but it doesn't attack e3. Bishop on c3? It attacks d4, e5, f6 — not e3. Bishop on g2? Attacks h1, f3, e4 — not e3. So e3 is undefended.\n\nTherefore, **N: g4-e3** delivers a **discovered attack** by the bishop on c8 (BBF0) along the c8-h3 diagonal, but wait — does the knight move uncover the bishop?\n\ng4 is on g4, knight moving from g4 to e3 — what diagonal does that open? The knight was blocking nothing for the bishop on c8. The c8 bishop points toward f1: c8-d7-e6-f5-g4-h3 — oh! **g4 is on the diagonal from c8 to h3**. There is a White knight on g4? No — it's my own knight on g4. So my knight was on g4, which is on the c8-h3 diagonal. So when I move the knight away, **I open the diagonal from c8 to h3**, and since h3 is adjacent to f1 (diagonally), but more importantly, **the bishop on c8 now attacks h3, g4, f5, e6, d7, and crucially — if the path is clear — all the way to f1?**\n\nc8 → d7 → e6 → f5 → g4 → h3 — that’s the direction. But f1 is not on that diagonal. The diagonal c8-h3 is a8-h1? No: c8 to h3 is a diagonal going up-right: each step +file, +rank. From c8 (file c, rank 8) to d7 (d,7)? No — that’s down-right. Actually, from c8, going down-right is d7, e6, f5, g4, h3 — yes. So the bishop on c8 attacks h3 via that diagonal.\n\nBut f1 is not on that diagonal. f1 is two files left and two ranks down from h3 — not aligned.\n\nSo no direct check from the bishop after N: g4-e3.\n\nBut the **knight fork on e3** attacks both **king on f1** and **queen on d1** — that’s the key.\n\nYes: knight on e3 attacks:\n- f1 (king)\n- d1 (queen)\n- f5, d5, c4, c2, etc.\n\nSo White will lose material — either king is exposed (but must move), or queen is lost.\n\nAfter N: g4-e3, White is in **check** from the knight? Yes — king on f1 is attacked by knight on e3.\n\nSo White must respond to check.\n\nOptions:\n- King moves: f1-e1, f1-g1, f1-e2, f1-g2 — but g1 is attacked by my knight on e3? e3 to g1 is +2 file, -2 rank