f check**.\n\nMust escape check.\n\nOptions:\n\n1. Move king to f8 (K: g8-f8) — only king move listed.\n\n2. Capture the knight on f6.\n\nCan any Black piece capture f6?\n\n- e3: NBF1 → Black knight on e3. Can it go to f5? Yes — N: e3-f5 → f5, not f6.\n\ne3 to f6? e3 to f6 is 1 file, 3 ranks — not an L-shape.\n\nL-shape: ±2 files & ±1 rank, or ±1 file & ±2 ranks.\n\ne3 to f6: Δfile=+1, Δrank=+3 → invalid.\n\ne3 to d5: +1,-2? e3 to d5: file -1, rank +2 → yes, but not listed.\n\nBut valid moves include N: e3-f5, g4, g2, etc. — no e3xf6.\n\nSo knight cannot capture.\n\nBishop on b2: can it reach f6? b2 to f6: same diagonal? b2 (dark square), f6 (light square) — no, different color.\n\nc8 bishop: c8 to f6? c8 (dark), f6 (light) — no.\n\nd4 pawn: d4 to e5 or c5? not f6.\n\ng7 pawn: g7 to f6? diagonally — yes, if attacking.\n\ng7 to f6: file -1, rank -1 → diagonal capture.\n\nIs f6 occupied by enemy? Yes — White knight.\n\nSo P: g7-f6 is a capture.\n\nBut is it in the valid moves list?\n\nValid moves: P: g7-g5, g7-g6 — but not g7-f6.\n\nWhy not?\n\nBecause g7-f6 would be a capture, but only if it\'s legal.\n\nBut g7 is PBF0 — can move g7-g6 or g7-g5 (two squares option).\n\nBut capture is diagonal — f6 is one square diagonally forward-left.\n\nBut is it in the list? No — not listed.\n\nSimilarly, f7 pawn: f7 to e6 or g6? not f6.\n\nOnly way to capture f6 is via g7xf6 or maybe queen?\n\nh8 queen: h8 to f6? h8 to f6: file -2, rank -2 — diagonal, yes, but is it clear?\n\nh8 to f6: passes through g7.\n\ng7 has PBF0 — Black pawn — so blocked.\n\nSo queen cannot go to f6.\n\na8 rook? a8 to f8? f8 is empty — but then doesn’t block or capture.\n\nBut rook on a8 can go to f8? Only if horizontal — but a8 to f8 is rank 8, files a to f.\n\nBut b8, c8, d8, e8, f8: b8 empty, c8 BBF0, d8 empty, e8 empty, f8 NWF1 — so path blocked by c8 bishop.\n\nSo rook cannot move far.\n\nBut even if it could, not to f6.\n\nSo only ways to escape check:\n\n1. King moves: only listed move is K: g8-f8\n\n2. Capture the knight on f6.\n\nPossible capturers:\n\n- g7 pawn: g7xf6 — but not in valid moves list\n\n- Queen h8: h8-f6? h8 to f6: diagonal, but g7 is occupied by own pawn → blocked\n\n- Knight e3: no move to f6\n\n- Bishop b2: b2 to f6? b2 (dark), f6 (light) — wrong color\n\n- Bishop c8: c8 to f6? c8 (dark), f6 (light) — no\n\n- d4 pawn: d4 to e5 or c5 — not f6\n\nSo no piece can capture the knight on f6.\n\nBlock the check? Knight gives direct check — cannot be blocked, only captured or king moves.\n\nSo only legal move is to move the king.\n\nAvailable: K: g8-f8\n\nIs f8 safe?\n\nf8 has NWF1 — White knight.\n\nSo f8 is occupied by enemy piece.\n\ng8 to f8: is that a capture?\n\nYes — king captures knight on f6? No — f8.\n\ng8 to f8: adjacent square.\n\nf8 has NWF1 — White knight.\n\nSo Black king can capture the White knight on f8.\n\nIs that allowed? Only if f8 is under attack or not — but king can capture if the square is not defended.\n\nBut more importantly: is K: g8-f8 a capture?\n\nYes — moving to f8, which is occupied.\n\nBut in the move list, it\'s listed as "K: g8-f8" — likely implies capture.\n\nAnd since it\'s in the valid moves list, it must be legal.\n\nNow, after Kx f8, is the king still in check?\n\nAfter capturing on f8, king is on f8.\n\nIs f8 attacked?\n\nCheck for threats:\n\n- White has bishop on g2 (BWF1) — light-squared bishop.\n\ng2 to f8: g2 (g-file,2), f8 (f-file,8) — diagonal? g2 to f8: file -1, rank +6 — direction: northwest.\n\nSquares: g2, f3, e4, d5, c6, b7, a8 — f8 is not on that diagonal.\n\nf8 is f-file,8 — g2 is g,2 — difference: file -1, rank +6 — slope = 6, not 1 — not diagonal.\n\nSo bishop on g2 does not attack f8.\n\nWhite knight on f6? f6 to f8? same file — not knight move.\n\nf6 to e8, g8, h7, d7, d5, e4, g4, h5 — not f8.\n\nSo no knight attack.\n\nWhite queen? Not visible — h1? Not shown.\n\nBut no immediate threat to f8.\n\nWhite pawn on e3 (PWF0) — can it attack f4? But f8 is far.\n\nSo likely f8 is safe after capt