\n1 |RWF1|    |    |    |    |BWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a8-a7, R: a8-a6, R: a8-b8, B: b2-a1, B: b2-c1, B: b2-a3, B: b2-c3, P: b5-a4, P: b5-c4, P: b5-b4, B: c8-b7, B: c8-a6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d4-d3, N: d5-b4, N: d5-b6, N: d5-c3, N: d5-c7, N: d5-e3, N: d5-e7, N: d5-f4, N: d5-f6, P: e4-e3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-h7, Q: h8-h7, Q: h8-h6, Q: h8-h5, Q: h8-h4, Q: h8-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on g8 (KBF1), having already moved, so castling is not possible. The white king is on g1 (KWF1), also having moved. My position has several pieces underdeveloped, but there is a strong knight on d5 (NBF1) that is well-placed in the center and exerts pressure. White has advanced pawns on the queenside (a4, c3, d4) and a bishop on f2 (BWF1). There’s a pawn tension on the e-file with pawns on e4 (PWF1) and d5 potentially vulnerable.\n\nKey observations:\n- My queen on h8 is passive but can become active via h7 or h6.\n- The black pawn on g7 (PBF0) is still on its starting square; pushing it to g5 opens lines for the rook and queen on the h-file.\n- The knight on d5 is strong but could be attacked soon by White’s pieces.\n- White has a bishop on b2 (BBF1) and another on f2 (BWF1), both eyeing central squares.\n- Pushing g7-g5 immediately activates the h8 rook and queen along the h-file and prepares potential kingside activity.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: g7-g6 – Solid, prepares development of the bishop, but passive.\n- P: g7-g5 – Aggressive, opens the h-file for rook and queen, increases attacking chances on the kingside where White’s king is stuck.\n- N: d5-b4 – Attacks c2 and pressures White’s center, but may be met with tactics involving c3 or b3.\n- N: d5-e3 – Fork threat on c2? But White can likely respond with Bxe3.\n- Q: h8-h7 – Connects with rook and eyes h2, but less urgent than opening the file.\n- R: a8-a7 – Passive, no clear target.\n- B: c8-g4 – Possible pin on Nf3 if it existed, but no such knight; currently not useful.\n\nThe most dynamic and promising move is **P: g7-g5**, which opens the h-file and enables rapid deployment of heavy pieces. It creates immediate threats along the h-file and supports future ...Rh8-h6 or even ...Qh8-h4 ideas. It also prevents White from comfortably advancing h2-h4 without weakening.\n\nReason: Opening the h-file with tempo and activating the rook and queen outweighs slow development. It takes initiative at a moment when White may be consolidating.\n\nAnswer: P: g7-g5'}
