ce at b2 is BBF1 — that\'s *my* bishop (Black Bishop, never moved? But it can\'t be — it\'s on b2 which is White\'s side). Hold on — critical observation:\n\nWait — **BBF1 on b2**? That would mean a **Black Bishop** on b2. But b2 is on White\'s side of the board. Is that possible?\n\nYes — if the bishop moved there earlier. But then why is it marked F0 (never moved)? Contradiction. Let\'s reevaluate.\n\nActually, the board shows:\n- b2: BBF1 → Black Bishop, en passant flag F (not moved 2 steps), Moved flag F (has NOT moved)? But it\'s on b2, which is a starting square for White\'s bishop. Black’s bishops start on c8/f8. So BBF1 on b2 must have moved to get there — so "Moved" should be T. But it\'s labeled F. This suggests the "Moved" flag may not reflect actual movement history but rather castling eligibility, etc.\n\nBut more importantly — **the list of valid moves includes "B: b2-a1", etc., which implies that the engine considers the piece on b2 as movable by me — so it must be *my* piece**. Therefore, despite being on b2, it is indeed a **Black bishop**, possibly from promotion or advanced play.\n\nBut looking again: on a2 is RWF1 — White rook. So White has pieces on a2 and b2. Then how can I have a bishop on b2? Conflict.\n\nWait — no: the board says:\n- a2: RWF1 → White rook\n- b2: BBF1 → Black bishop\n\nSo both exist? Yes — different files. a2 = White rook, b2 = Black bishop. So a Black bishop is on b2 — plausible if it was developed there earlier.\n\nBut now the **valid moves list includes**:  \n"B: b2-a1, B: b2-c1, ..." — these are moves *available to me*, meaning the piece on b2 is mine — yes, BBF1 is Black, so I control it.\n\nBut also, I have another bishop on c4 (BBF1) — so two Black bishops? Yes, possibly one promoted.\n\nWait — c4: BBF1 → Black Bishop. So two Black bishops: one on b2, one on c4? But only one is listed in the move list as moving.\n\nLooking at the valid moves:  \nMoves listed include:\n- B: b2-a1, b2-c1, ..., (all from b2)\n- B: c4-b3, c4-a2, ... (from c4)\n\nAh — so both bishops are mine and can move.\n\nAlso, P: e3-e2 — my pawn on e3 moving to e2.\n\nAnd crucially: **R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, ..., R: f1-h1** — my rook on f1 can move along the first rank.\n\nWhite king is on g1 — exposed on the back rank.\n\nIf I play **R: f1-f2**, I lose the rook to Kxf2 — not good.\n\nBut if I play **R: f1-g1+**, that’s **check**! And it\'s in the move list: **R: f1-g1**\n\nThat would attack the White king directly.\n\nIs it legal? Let\'s see:\n- Rook on f1, moves to g1 — horizontally, yes.\n- No pieces between f1 and g1? f1, g1 — yes, adjacent. White king on g1 — so capturing it? No — you don\'t capture the king, but deliver check.\n\nBut wait — is g1 occupied? Yes — by KWF1. So **R: f1-g1** means **Rf1xg1+**, capturing the White king’s guard? But the king is there — so it would be **capture on g1**, but only if the king isn\'t defended.\n\nIs the White king on g1 defended?\n\nWhite has:\n- Rook on a2 (RWF1) — not guarding g1\n- Bishop on b2 (BBF1) — Black\'s piece? No — b2 is BBF1 — Black Bishop — so not White\'s!\n\nWait — confusion again.\n\nLet’s clarify piece ownership:\n\nFrom the board:\n- a2: RWF1 → **White** Rook\n- b2: BBF1 → **Black** Bishop\n- c4: BBF1 → **Black** Bishop\n- e3: PBF1 → **Black** Pawn\n- f1: RBF1 → **Black** Rook\n- g1: KWF1 → **White** King\n- g8: KBF1 → **Black** King\n- Others: various pawns\n\nSo White has:\n- King: g1\n- Rook: a2\n- And pawns: f4 (PWF1), and maybe others? h4: PBF1 → Black pawn? No — h4: PBF1 → **Black** Pawn? But h4 is near White’s side.\n\nWait — h4: PBF1 → Black pawn on h4? Yes — possible if advanced.\n\nBut who owns what?\n\nList of non-empty squares:\n- a2: RWF1 → White Rook\n- b2: BBF1 → Black Bishop\n- c4: BBF1 → Black Bishop\n- c5: PBF1 → Black Pawn\n- e3: PBF1 → Black Pawn\n- f1: RBF1 → Black Rook\n- f4: PWF1 → White Pawn\n- g1: KWF1 → White King\n- g8: KBF1 → Black King\n- h4: PBF1 → Black Pawn\n- a4: PBF1 → Black Pawn\n\nSo White has only three pieces: rook 