ve attacks d2, which is one square away from the white king (g1). More importantly, d2 is a critical central square close to the king, and this move threatens **check on d1** next move if not stopped. This could lead to forced sequences.\n- Q: a5-e1 – Directly delivers check to e1, but e1 is guarded by the black bishop on b2? No — wait: the bishop on b2 is **Black\'s own piece** (BBF1), so it does *not* guard e1. However, e1 is adjacent to KWF1 on g1? No — e1 is two files away. Actually, the White king is on g1, so e1 is not adjacent. But is there any piece guarding e1? Looking at the board: RWF1 on a1, PWF1 on g4, PBF1 on h4, etc. — **e1 is completely undefended**. So Q: a5-e1 is **check**, and since the king cannot capture the queen, and no other piece can interpose or capture, this may be decisive.\n\nWait: Can the queen move from a5 to e1? That’s a diagonal move: a5 → b4 → c3 → d2 → e1. Yes, it’s a diagonal (a5-e1 is the a5-e1 diagonal). So yes, it’s a legal queen move.\n\nAnd is e1 attacked? The White king is on g1. The queen moving to e1 gives **check** because it’s on the same rank as g1? No — e1 and g1 are both on rank 1, but the queen is coming from a5 to e1 — that’s a diagonal move, not horizontal. Wait: a5 to e1 is diagonal (difference of 4 files right, 4 ranks down — yes, that’s a diagonal). From e1, the queen **does not attack g1 horizontally** unless she moves to f1 or h1.\n\nBut wait — after Q:a5-e1, is the white king in check? Let\'s compute:\n\n- Queen on e1. King on g1. They are on the same rank (rank 1), two squares apart (f1 between them). So the queen **does control f1 and g1 from e1** — yes! A queen on e1 attacks g1 along the first rank.\n\nSo **Q: a5-e1 delivers check to the white king on g1**.\n\nCan the king escape? From g1, possible escapes: f1, h1, f2, g2, h2.\n\nCheck if those are attacked:\n- f1: Is it attacked? Currently, we have a black bishop on b2 — b2 to f1? b2-c3-d4-e5-f6 — no, not the same diagonal. b2 to f1 is b2-c1? No, that’s backwards. b2 to f1: difference of +4 files, -1 rank — not diagonal. So bishop doesn\'t attack f1. Queen on e1 controls f1 (same rank). So f1 is attacked.\n- h1: e1 to h1 is three right — queen controls it. So h1 is attacked.\n- f2: e1 to f2 is diagonal (+1,+1) — yes, queen controls f2.\n- g2: e1 to g2 is +2 files, +1 rank — not aligned. Not attacked by queen directly.\n- h2: e1 to h2 is +3, +1 — not aligned.\n\nSo only f1, f2, g1, h1 are attacked on rank/file/diagonals from e1. g2 is not attacked.\n\nSo king can go to g2? Is g2 occupied? Current board: g2 is empty. Yes.\n\nSo after Q: a5-e1+, White king can play K: g1-g2, escaping check.\n\nThen what? We don’t have immediate follow-up for mate. But we’ve forced the king and gained tempo.\n\nHowever, better: **Q: a5-d2**.\n\nWhy? d2 is a dark square. Queen from a5 to d2: a5→b4→c3→d2, yes — diagonal, valid.\n\nQueen on d2:\n- Controls d2, which is adjacent to e1 and c1, etc.\n- Most importantly: **d2 is one square diagonally from g1**? No — d2 to g1 is +3 files, -1 rank — not aligned.\nBut wait: from d2, the queen **attacks along the second rank**? No, d2 is rank 2, king is on g1 (rank 1).\nDiagonally: d2 to g1 is d2-e1-f2-g1? No — d2-e1 is diagonal, but e1 to f2 is opposite direction. Actually, d2 to g1 is not a straight line.\n\nSo d2 does **not** attack g1.\n\nBut earlier analysis was wrong: **a5 to e1 is NOT a check**, because a5 to e1 is diagonal, but from e1 to g1 is horizontal — yes, same rank! e1 and g1 are both on rank 1. So queen on e1 attacks g1 along the rank.\n\nYes — so **Q: a5-e1 is check**, because queen controls the entire rank 1.\n\nAnd king must move.\n\nAs above, king can go to g2 (not attacked by anything else), so not checkmate.\n\nBut still, it forces the king into the center and disrupts development.\n\nBut even better: **Q: a5-c3** — let\'s reconsider.\n\nc3 is a light square. a5 to c3: a5-b4-c3, diagonal down-right, yes.\n\nFrom c3, queen attacks:\n- The center\n- b2 is our own bishop, so no issue\n- Critically: **c3 to g1 is a diagonal**: c3-d2-e1-f2-g1? No — c3 to g1 is +4 files, -2 ranks — slope = -0.5, not ±1. Not diagonal.\n\nBut **d2** is key.\n\nWait — **Q: a5-d2**:\n\nFrom d2, queen is on d2.\n\nNow, **d2 to g1** is +3 files, -1 rank — not diagonal, not same rank/file — so no attack.\n\nBut look at **Q: a5-b4**: b4.\n\nFrom b4, queen attacks:\n- a5 to b4 is one step.\n- From b4, can she attack g1? b4 to g1: +5, -3 — no.\nNot helpful.\n\nWait — best attacking square: **Q: a5-h5**? Not in list.\n\nWait — valid moves include: Q: a5-e1, Q: a5-d2, Q: a5-c3, etc.\n\nBut **Q: a5-d8**? That\'s going to d8 — far from action.\n\nNo.\n\nWait — another idea: **B: b2-g7**?\n\nThat’s a long diagonal: b2 to g7 — yes, diagonal of +5, +5 — valid.\n\nFrom g7, the bishop would be on g7, which is adjacent to h8 and f8, and importantly, **on the diagonal pointing directly at the white king on g1**? g7 to g1 is vertical? Yes — same file!\n\ng7 to g1 is the g-file. Bishop moves diagonally, not vertically. So **bishop cannot attack along the g-file**.\n\nSo no.\n\nBut **queen to d2**:\n\nLet’s reevaluate: Q: a5-d2\n\nFrom d2, queen controls:\n- Entire d-file\n- Entire rank 2\n- Diagonals from d2\n\nBut most importantly: **if the queen goes to d2, then next move, if not captured, can go to d1 or e1 or c1, delivering check**.\n\nBut more promising: **Q: a5-e1+**, as established, is **check**, and forces the king to move.\n\nAfter Ke1 (no, king is on g1), Kg1-g2, then we can play **Qe1-e2+**, checking again, forcing the king back.\n\nBut White might play other moves.\n\nAlternatively, consider **immediate mating net ideas**.\n\nLook at **Q: a5-a6**\n\nNot urgent.\n\nOr **K: g8-g7**, centralizing king? But not necessary yet.\n\nBest candidate: **Q: a5-e1+**\n\nIt’s a check, gains tempo, and disrupts the opponent.\n\nWhite king must move to g2 (only safe square: f1, f2, h1, h2 are all attacked by queen on e1 via rank or diagonal).\n\nAfter **Kg1-g2**, we play **Qe1-e2+**, check again.\n\nThen king must go to g1 or g3 or h2 or f2.\n\ng1 is attacked by queen on e2? e2 to g1: +2, -1 — not aligned. No.\n\nf2: e2 to f2 — same rank, yes, attacked.\n\nh2: e2 to h2 — +3, 0 — same rank, yes.\n\ng3: e2 to g3 — +2, +1 — not aligned.\n\nSo king can go to g3.\n\nBut g3 is empty? Yes.\n\nSo K: g2-g3.\n\nThen we don’t have immediate mate.\n\nBut we’re chasing the king.\n\nAlternatively, after Q: a5-e1+, suppose White doesn’t know and plays poorly.\n\nBut we want strongest move.\n\nAnother option: **Q: a5-d2**, not check, but prepares **Qd2-d1#** next move?\n\nd1 is occupied? No — d1 is empty. a1 has RWF1, but d1 is empty.\n\nFrom d2 to d1 is one square down — same file.\n\nIf queen is on d2, and moves to d1, is that check? d1 is not adjacent to g1. d1 to g1 is three files over — same rank? d1 and g1 are both rank 1.\n\nYes! So queen on d1 attacks g1 along rank 1.\n\nSo **Q: d2-d1+** is check.\n\nBut can be blocked or king moves.\n\nBut White can play **after Q: a5-d2**, simply **move the king to h1 or f1**, and we don’t gain much.\n\nBut if we play **Q: a5-e1+**, we force the king to move immediately.\n\nMoreover, **e1 is deeper into enemy territory**, and the queen is centralized.\n\nAlso, **e1 is supported by no one**, but after the queen lands, it’s hard to dislodge.\n\nBut White can capture the queen? Who can capture on e1?\n\n- a1 rook? On a1, same rank — yes, rook on a1 can capture on e1? a1 to e1 is +4 files, same rank — yes, rook moves horizontally.\n\nOh! **RWF1 on a1 can capture the queen on e1**.\n\nSo if we play Q: a5-e1, White can respond with **R: a1xe1**, capturing the queen.\n\nThen we lose our queen for free.\n\nSo **Q: a5-e1 is a blunder** — loses queen to rook on a1.\n\nCannot do that.\n\nSimilarly, **Q: a5-d2** — can it be captured?\n\nd2: who attacks d2?\n\n- a1 rook: a1 to d2 — same file? No, different file and rank. a1 to d2 is +3, +1 — not aligned. So rook doesn\'t attack d2.\n- Any other piece? No.\n\nSo queen on d2 is safe.\n\nFrom d2, it attacks:\n- d1, d3, d4, etc.\n- Rank 2: c2, b2, a2, e2, f2, g2, h2\n\nMost importantly: **g2 is attacked by queen on d2**? d2 to g2 is +3, 0 — same rank, yes.\n\ng2 is where the king might flee.\n\nBut also: **can we deliver check?** Not immediately, because g1 is not attacked from d2.\n\nBut **next move, we can play Q: d2-d1+**, check.\n\nThen king must move to f1, f2, h1, h2, g2.\n\nBut d1 attacks entire rank 1 and d-file.\n\nAfter Q: d2-d1+, king cannot stay on rank 1 except f1/h1/f2/h2/g2, but f1 and h1 are on rank 1, attacked.\n\nSo king must go to f2, g2, h2.\n\nSuppose K: g1-f2.\n\nThen we might follow up with B: b2-e5 or something.\n\nBut better: after Q: a5-d2, we prepare a strong threat.\n\nOther options: **Q: a5-c3**\n\nc3: from a5 to c3 — diagonal.\n\nIs c3 attacked by anything? White has no minor pieces developed.\n\nQueen on c3 attacks:\n- c-file\n- rank 3\n- diagonal to g7, etc.\n- importantly, **c3 to g1** is not aligned.\n\nBut **c3 to a1** — same diagonal? b2 is in between — our bishop.\n\nNot useful.\n\n**Q: a5-b4** — similar.\n\nBut **Q: a5-d8** — too far.\n\nBest quiet move: **Q: a5-a6** — advances, keeps queen on file, pressures a1 rook indirectly.\n\nBut not aggressive.\n\nWait — **B: b2-g7** — long diagonal.\n\nb2 to g7: +5 files, +5 ranks — yes, diagonal.\n\nFrom g7, bishop is on g7.\n\nNow, **g7 to g1** — same file? Yes, but bishop doesn\'t move vertically.\n\nNo.\n\nBut **g7 to a1** — diagonal? g7-f6-e5-d4-c3-b2-a1 — yes! That\'s the long diagonal.\n\nAnd on a1 is **RWF1**, the white rook.\n\nSo **B: b2-g7** attacks the rook on a1.\n\nWhite must defend or move it.\n\nIf we play B: b2-g7, then we threaten to capture the rook.\n\nWhite might play R: a1-a2 or something.\n\nWe win a rook for a bishop.\n\nMaterial gain: rook vs bishop — favorable.\n\nCurrently, we have queen and bishop and pawns vs their rook and king and pawns.\n\nLosing the rook hurts White.\n\nSo **B: b2-g7** is a strong move — threatens material gain.\n\nAre there any defenses?\n\nAfter B: b2-g7, White can:\n- Move the rook: R: a1-a2, a3, a4, etc.\n- Or block the diagonal? Only if they play a piece on c3, d4, etc., but no pieces there.\n- Or ignore, but we capture.\n\nSo likely, they lose the rook or retreat.\n\nEven if they retreat, we\'ve gained time and material pressure.\n\nCompare to queen moves.\n\n**Q: a5-d2** is also good — centralizes queen, prepares d1 invasion.\n\nBut **B: b2-g7** wins material immediately if not addressed.\n\nMoreover, after B: b2-g7, if White doesn\'t respond, we play B: g7xa1, winning the rook.\n\nSo it\'s a direct threat.\n\nTherefore, **B: b2-g7** is very strong.\n\nBut is it in the valid moves?\n\nValid moves: "B: b2-g7" — yes, listed.\n\nAlso, queen moves like Q: a5-d2 are good, but not as immediately punishing.\n\nAnother bishop move: **B: c4-f1** — attacks f1, which is near king.\n\nf1 is adjacent to g1.\n\nSo B: c4-f1 would attack f1, which is a flight square for the king.\n\nBut not a direct threat.\n\nWhereas B: b2-g7 attacks a valuable piece.\n\nSo better.\n\nPawns: advancing pawns won\'t help much.\n\nKing moves: premature.\n\nSo top candidates:\n1. **B: b2-g7** — threatens rook on a1\n2. **Q: a5-d2** — prepares centralization and future attack\n\nBut **B: b2-g7** creates an immediate tactical threat.\n\nWhite has no way to prevent the loss of the rook without conceding positional weaknesses.\n\nTherefore, best move.\n\nReason: It exploits the undefended rook on a1 by placing the bishop on the long diagonal, threatening to capture it on the next move. This gains material or forces White into passive defense, significantly increasing Black\'s winning chances.\n\nAnswer: B: b2-g7'}
