\n1 |    |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a3, P: c5-c4, P: e4-e3, B: f7-e6, B: f7-d5, B: f7-c4, B: f7-b3, B: f7-a2, B: f7-g6, B: f7-h5, B: f7-e8, B: g7-f6, B: g7-e5, B: g7-d4, B: g7-c3, B: g7-b2, B: g7-a1, B: g7-h6, B: g7-f8, B: g7-h8, K: g8-f8, K: g8-h7, K: g8-h8, P: h4-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king is on g8, still in the corner, while White’s king is dangerously exposed on f2—deep in the center and far from safety. White has no pieces developed, and their only active unit near the center is a pawn on f4. I have two bishops already out, on f7 and g7, both poised to exert pressure along diagonals. My pawn on c5 is ready to advance, and pawns on a4 and h4 can push forward. The White king on f2 is particularly vulnerable to diagonal attacks, especially from my dark-squared bishop on g7 (g7-c3 or g7-e5) or the light-squared bishop on f7 targeting e6 or d5.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **B: g7-e5** – Strong. Targets the weak f2 square indirectly via the diagonal; prepares potential discovered attack if the other bishop moves.\n- **B: g7-d4** – Very strong. Directly attacks f2 with check! This is a discovered attack candidate because moving this bishop unleases the f7 bishop's diagonal to f2.\nWait — actually, B: g7-d4 would place the bishop on d4, which *does* attack f2? Let's check: d4 to f2 is a diagonal (d4-e3-f2), yes — so **B: g7-d4 delivers check** to the White king on f2!\n\nThat is critical.\n\nSimilarly:\n- **B: f7-e6+** – Also checks the king (e6-f2 diagonal), but less forcing.\n- **B: g7-b2** – Attacks the rook on c1 (if it were there), but currently not impactful.\n- **P: c5-c4** – Solid, gains space, but passive compared to tactical shots.\n- **B: g7-d4** – Immediate **check**, attacks king, forces response, and may win material or lead to mate.\n\nLet’s verify: g7 to d4 is three squares left and three down — that’s a valid diagonal move for a bishop. And d4–e3–f2 is indeed a diagonal. So **B: g7-d4+** is check.\n\nWhite king on f2 cannot capture, and escape squares are limited: f2 → e3, e2, f1, g1, g2, e1, g3. But many of those may be covered.\n\nAfter **B: g7-d4+**, White must respond to check. Possible replies: Kf2-e3, Kf2-g1, Kf2-g3, Kf2-e1, or Kf2-e2 — but all are awkward. Meanwhile, I threaten follow-ups like ...Bf7-e6+, or even ...Q-side development.\n\nMoreover, after B: g7-d4, the bishop is protected by the f7 bishop? No — but it doesn’t need to be immediately. The threat is immediate damage.\n\nBut wait — is **B: g7-d4** even in the valid move list?\n\nYes: Valid moves include: **B: g7-d4**\n\nSo this is a legal, powerful check.\n\nOther checks?\n- B: f7-e6+ — also possible. e6 to f2 is diagonal. Yes, that’s also check.\n\nBut **B: g7-d4+** attacks directly and occupies a central square, while **B: f7-e6+** is more passive.\n\nAlso, **B: g7-d4** threatens more — it could support a quick ...c5-c4 or even prepare ...Qa5+ ideas later.\n\nBut let’s compare:\n\n- **B: g7-d4+**: Immediate centralization, check, forces king to move, possibly into danger.\n- **B: f7-e6+**: Check, but e6 is closer to the edge, less central, and White king can go to e3 or g3 more easily.\n\nTherefore, **B: g7-d4** is the strongest move.\n\nReason: It delivers check to the exposed White king on f2, exploits the lack of development and poor king safety, forces a weakening move, and increases pressure in the center. It’s one of the few moves that create immediate threats, and it maximizes winning chances by capitalizing on White’s premature king position.\n\nAnswer: B: g7-d4"}
