\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |RWF1|KWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a3, B: c4-b3, B: c4-a2, B: c4-d3, B: c4-e2, B: c4-f1, B: c4-b5, B: c4-a6, B: c4-d5, B: c4-e6, B: c4-f7, B: d4-c3, B: d4-b2, B: d4-a1, B: d4-e5, B: d4-f6, B: d4-g7, B: d4-h8, P: e3-e2, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-g7, K: g8-h7, K: g8-h8, P: h4-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, with a significant material advantage in the form of two bishops on the board and a king that is relatively safe. The White king is on g1 (KWF1), and the only active White piece is the rook on g1 (RWF1), which has not moved. My king on g8 is currently not under threat. There are several pawns on both sides, but most are static or blocked. Notably, my pawn on e3 (PBF1) is one square away from promoting if it can advance. However, it is blocked by no pieces directly, and advancing it to e2 opens up promotion next turn if unimpeded. Additionally, my bishops have strong diagonal control, especially targeting key central and queenside squares.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: e3-e2** – This move advances the pawn one step from e3 to e2, bringing it to the penultimate rank. Next move, it will promote to any piece (likely a queen), delivering a decisive material advantage. It’s fast, direct, and hard for White to stop unless they use the rook or king, both of which are distant.\n- **B: c4-b3** – Attacks the undefended pawn on a4? No — actually a4 is a black pawn (PBF1). This move doesn’t gain material. It may support control of central squares but is less urgent.\n