l central dark squares. Moving to b3 cuts off the king’s escape. But capturing may not be urgent.\n- **Bishop on d4**: Has strong diagonals toward the white king, especially f2 and g1. B:d4-g1 attacks the weak h2 square indirectly.\n- **Pawn on g3**: Pushing g3-g2 prepares queening next move if unopposed, which is very strong. After g2, the pawn threatens promotion on g1=Q, which would likely decide the game.\n- **King moves**: Safe but not urgent; better to resolve tactical threats first.\n\nThe most forcing and decisive move appears to be **g3-g2**, advancing the pawn one step from promotion. After this, on the next turn, it will promote to a queen (or other piece) unless stopped. White has no immediate way to stop it — their king is too far, and they have no pieces nearby. This creates an unstoppable threat.\n\nReason: Promoting the pawn is the fastest path to a decisive material advantage. Even if White uses their next move to try to counter (e.g., K:h3-g3 to block), I can promote with check or support with queen. No other move offers such a concrete win.\n\nAnswer: P: g3-g2"}
