g might be risky without sufficient cover; also, king moves don’t generate immediate initiative.\n- K: e5-d5 / d6 / e6 / f6 – Some are safe, but again, passive compared to active piece play.\n\nThe most dynamic and threatening move is **B: c4-f1**, placing the bishop deep into white’s territory on a critical square. It eyes potential tactics involving the back rank, especially if White’s king remains uncastled or slow to develop.\n\nReason: Active piece play is crucial when the opponent’s king is still in the center. Placing the bishop on f1 exerts immediate strategic pressure on White’s position, particularly targeting weak points around the king’s starting zone. It limits White’s development and could lead to tactical opportunities, especially since f1 is a key square for rook or queen activity. This move maximizes activity and initiative.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-f1'}
