iagonal.\n\nSo after Qh8-h1:\n- White king on f1 is in check (attacked by queen on h1)\n- Possible escapes:\n   - f1 → f2: attacked by Qh1 (diagonal)\n   - f1 → g1: attacked by Qh1 (same rank)\n   - f1 → g2: attacked by Qh1 (diagonal: h1 to g2 is one left, one up)\n- No friendly piece can block — no white pieces near\n- Cannot capture the queen — no white piece attacks h1\n\nTherefore, **Qh8-h1 is checkmate**.\n\nThis is a forced win.\n\nOther queen moves do not end the game immediately.\n\nKing moves (e.g., e5-d5, etc.) improve positioning but waste a winning opportunity.\n\nPawn move a4-a3 is slow and irrelevant.\n\nReason: The optimal move is to deliver immediate checkmate. Q: h8-h1 achieves this by placing the black queen on h1, attacking the white king on f1 with no legal escapes, resulting in victory.\n\nAnswer: Q: h8-h1"}
