\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: e5-d4, K: e5-d5, K: e5-d6, K: e5-e4, K: e5-e6, K: e5-f4, K: e5-f5, K: e5-f6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: The board is nearly empty, with only two kings remaining: Black's king on e5 and White's king on h2. Black is to move. The goal is to checkmate White or force a stalemate, but since there are no pawns or other pieces, the only possibility is to checkmate with the king—but that is impossible with just two kings. However, as Black, my objective is to avoid stalemate while trying to win; yet in this position, neither side can deliver checkmate. The game will likely end in a draw unless one side blunders into stalemate.\n\nHowever, White’s king is on h2 (corner), and if not careful, White could be stalemated. My job is to avoid any move that would lead to stalemate immediately or unnecessarily reduce mobility.\n\nCurrently, the Black king on e5 has several legal moves. I must choose one that keeps the maximum number of future options open and avoids placing White’s king in a stalemate situation prematurely.\n\nEvaluation:\n- K: e5-d4 — Centralizes the king slightly, moving toward the center-left. Keeps good range.\n- K: e5-d5 — Symmetrical central control, good flexibility.\n- K: e5-d6 — Moves up, closer to top edge. Less ideal for controlling White’s movement.\n- K: e5-e4 — Moves down toward rank 4, closer to White’s king vertically.\n- K: e5-e6 — Upward, away from White’s king. Not ideal for pursuit.\n- K: e5-f4 — Approaches White’s king directly (g3/h3/f3 squares may become relevant). This cuts off White’s king’s escape toward g1/h1.\n- K: e5-f5 — Slightly better centralization than f4, still pressuring the kingside.\n- K: e5-f6 — Too far up, reduces pressure.\n\nBest options are those that restrict White’s king from safely moving and keep the opposition potential (e.g., f4 or f5).\n\nCritical observation: With only kings left, the side that forces the opponent into the corner *without* causing stalemate wins by driving the king in. But here, Black is active, and White is passive. By approaching, Black maintains winning chances (though technically a draw under insufficient material, we play as if aiming to checkmate).\n\nK: e5-f4 is strong because it:\n- Prepares to occupy g3 or f3\n- Controls g5, f4, e3 — key squares near h2\n- Reduces White’s space\n- Does not immediately threaten nothing but increases pressure\n\nBut wait: Is any move actually dangerous?\n\nWhite’s king is on h2. After most of these moves, White will have at least one legal move (e.g., h2-g2 or h2-h1), so no immediate stalemate.\n\nBut we must avoid moves like e5-f5? Let's see:\n\nAfter K: e5-f5, White plays K: h2-g2 — fine.\nAfter K: e5-f4, White plays K: h2-g2 — also fine.\n\nAll current moves are safe from stalemate.\n\nBut best plan: Approach with king. f4 gives closest approach with support for g3 later.\n\nReason: Moving to f4 brings the Black king closer to the corner where White’s king resides, maximizing future control and minimizing White’s mobility over time. It sets up the possibility of forcing the White king to h1 or h2 with fewer escape squares, potentially leading to beneficial positioning (even though draw is inevitable with perfect play, we maximize practical chances).\n\nAnswer: K: e5-f4"}
